{
  "_createdAt": "2026-04-27T21:47:33Z",
  "_id": "wp-post-1465-en",
  "_rev": "37hNSegHf4NtjSKr1qpspc",
  "_type": "article",
  "_updatedAt": "2026-07-05T16:14:41Z",
  "author": {
    "_ref": "author-legacy-archive",
    "_type": "reference"
  },
  "body": [
    {
      "_key": "000000000001",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "qo0792qlmmcu",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [
            "em"
          ],
          "text": "Egypt is not a single destination. It is the country of the pyramids, the country of the Nile, the country of the desert, the country of the Red Sea — and the country of "
        },
        {
          "_key": "bhtijjcrevp5",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [
            "em",
            "zavzpa96sbv7"
          ],
          "text": "Cairo"
        },
        {
          "_key": "y3ejga0fx49a",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [
            "em"
          ],
          "text": ", which is still moving as an enormous city."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [
        {
          "_key": "zavzpa96sbv7",
          "_type": "internalLink",
          "reference": {
            "_ref": "wp-page-83284",
            "_type": "reference"
          }
        }
      ],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000003",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "mkez0fs97905",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "First-time travellers often ask \"where should I go in Egypt.\" The answer is not simple, because Egypt is not made of one tone. The pyramids of "
        },
        {
          "_key": "dh481ly0jvu8",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [
            "h2quajxuvf99"
          ],
          "text": "Giza"
        },
        {
          "_key": "kelb2evlbi70",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": " and the temples of "
        },
        {
          "_key": "k72597jl9l3b",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [
            "5z071obpjlsi"
          ],
          "text": "Luxor"
        },
        {
          "_key": "cmdn3nxywhvw",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": " are both ancient Egypt, but the experience is completely different. The Nile at "
        },
        {
          "_key": "4ovagxvl4fvj",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [
            "komakuorv26y"
          ],
          "text": "Aswan"
        },
        {
          "_key": "gsrcnhdvicqg",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": " and the alleys of Cairo barely feel like the same country. Go to the Red Sea and history is replaced by clarity of water and the reef."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [
        {
          "_key": "h2quajxuvf99",
          "_type": "internalLink",
          "reference": {
            "_ref": "wp-page-58854",
            "_type": "reference"
          }
        },
        {
          "_key": "5z071obpjlsi",
          "_type": "internalLink",
          "reference": {
            "_ref": "wp-page-58877",
            "_type": "reference"
          }
        },
        {
          "_key": "komakuorv26y",
          "_type": "internalLink",
          "reference": {
            "_ref": "wp-page-58758",
            "_type": "reference"
          }
        }
      ],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000005",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "000000000004",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "So when you plan Egypt, deciding the spine of the trip matters more than \"including the famous places.\" Are you reading the ancient civilisation in depth, are you moving with the Nile, are you adding rest on the Red Sea, are you reaching out into the desert?"
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000007",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "000000000006",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "What follows are the main places worth knowing about for a first Egypt trip — or for a second pass that goes one layer deeper."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000009",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "000000000008",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "Cairo and Giza"
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "h2"
    },
    {
      "_key": "00000000000b",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "00000000000a",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "Cairo is Egypt's doorway and, simultaneously, the city that is least easy. Traffic is heavy, the sound is loud, the layers are thick. For understanding Egypt, it is unavoidable."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "00000000000d",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "00000000000c",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "The pyramids of Giza are, of course, the first major reason. The pyramids of Khufu, Khafre, and Menkaure with the Sphinx — even when you know them from photographs, the distance and weight are different in person. Here it becomes clear that ancient Egypt was not an abstract \"civilisation\" but an enormous machinery of stone, labour, surveying, and kingship."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "00000000000f",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "00000000000e",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "The Grand Egyptian Museum and the Egyptian Museum on Tahrir Square play different roles. The GEM is the place to see ancient Egypt — including Tutankhamun — inside a modern display environment. The older Tahrir museum is a building with its own history as the long-time centre of Egyptian archaeology. If time allows, both are worth seeing."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000011",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "z431x6p6ol1o",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "In Cairo, Islamic Cairo cannot be left out. "
        },
        {
          "_key": "cimkmnzjxmti",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [
            "1s7z4o9nq4ne"
          ],
          "text": "Khan el-Khalili"
        },
        {
          "_key": "589hyuw0irrf",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": ", al-Azhar, the Mosque of Sultan Hasan, the Citadel area. What you see here is not pharaonic Egypt; it is Cairo from the medieval period onward. It is essential for not reading Egypt as antiquity alone."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [
        {
          "_key": "1s7z4o9nq4ne",
          "_type": "internalLink",
          "reference": {
            "_ref": "guideArticle.cairo.khan-el-khalili",
            "_type": "reference"
          }
        }
      ],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000013",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "000000000012",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "Saqqara and Dahshur"
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "h2"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000015",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "000000000014",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "To think you understand pyramids from Giza alone is a little early. South of Cairo, Saqqara and Dahshur show that pyramids did not appear in completed form — they were the result of a long experiment."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000017",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "000000000016",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "Djoser's step pyramid at Saqqara is a turning point in monumental stone architecture. Unlike the smooth pyramids of Giza, its stepped form shows the developmental stage of royal tomb building."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000019",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "000000000018",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "At Dahshur, you can see Sneferu's Bent Pyramid and the Red Pyramid. The Bent Pyramid has its unusual change of angle mid-build; the Red Pyramid sits close to the completed true-pyramid form. With fewer visitors than Giza, you can read the architecture more quietly."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "00000000001b",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "00000000001a",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "Luxor"
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "h2"
    },
    {
      "_key": "00000000001d",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "00000000001c",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "Luxor is where Egypt's ancient history feels densest. The city is ancient Thebes — temples on the east bank, tombs and mortuary temples on the west."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "00000000001f",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "1h6xflhmnfxr",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "The east bank centres on Karnak and "
        },
        {
          "_key": "y9dnk2mnwz20",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [
            "ps0hrrhipfh1"
          ],
          "text": "Luxor Temple"
        },
        {
          "_key": "6hb7rqsydwo5",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": ". Karnak is less a single temple than an enormous religious complex added to over centuries. Walking through the hypostyle hall, you understand that the architecture was built to overwhelm the human. Luxor Temple stands inside the modern town and carries a strong sense of Egyptian time stacking on itself."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [
        {
          "_key": "ps0hrrhipfh1",
          "_type": "internalLink",
          "reference": {
            "_ref": "guideArticle.luxor.the-luxor-temple",
            "_type": "reference"
          }
        }
      ],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000021",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "vewhp8zf6z4l",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "The west bank holds the "
        },
        {
          "_key": "myfsm9woczv3",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [
            "l2cmra7eqkg3"
          ],
          "text": "Valley of the Kings"
        },
        {
          "_key": "wki77i7gfmu7",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": ", the Hatshepsut mortuary temple, "
        },
        {
          "_key": "q5x2hgjli90p",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [
            "qyh3rin6w5g6"
          ],
          "text": "Medinet Habu"
        },
        {
          "_key": "p66qi9d88jbn",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": ", the "
        },
        {
          "_key": "5musn3p2ij0c",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [
            "h964a62c97vq"
          ],
          "text": "Valley of the Queens"
        },
        {
          "_key": "7zbeymeei6h4",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": ", and "
        },
        {
          "_key": "pl37edpz62ue",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [
            "1ib7toosdtb2"
          ],
          "text": "Deir el-Medina"
        },
        {
          "_key": "5x7b7l9u60ag",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": ". The afterlife, kingship, the workers' village, and funerary architecture all sit inside one landscape."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [
        {
          "_key": "l2cmra7eqkg3",
          "_type": "internalLink",
          "reference": {
            "_ref": "guideArticle.luxor.valley-of-the-kings",
            "_type": "reference"
          }
        },
        {
          "_key": "qyh3rin6w5g6",
          "_type": "internalLink",
          "reference": {
            "_ref": "guideArticle.luxor.madinat-habu-temple",
            "_type": "reference"
          }
        },
        {
          "_key": "h964a62c97vq",
          "_type": "internalLink",
          "reference": {
            "_ref": "guideArticle.luxor.valley-of-the-queens",
            "_type": "reference"
          }
        },
        {
          "_key": "1ib7toosdtb2",
          "_type": "internalLink",
          "reference": {
            "_ref": "guideArticle.luxor.deir-el-madina",
            "_type": "reference"
          }
        }
      ],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000023",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "000000000022",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "Luxor is wasted as a day trip. Two nights minimum, three is better — you can see sites in the cool morning, rest at midday, and go out again in the late afternoon, the natural rhythm."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000025",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "000000000024",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "Aswan"
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "h2"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000027",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "000000000026",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "In Aswan the character of the Nile changes. Rocks appear in the river, islands scatter through it, and the air carries the southern light. The town is gentler in scale than Cairo or Luxor, and a very good place to feel the Nile."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000029",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "z83p8vy1laet",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "The Temple of Philae is a major reason to come to Aswan. Threatened by flooding after the "
        },
        {
          "_key": "3kdxwuesvfhy",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [
            "hos9ez8hysz4"
          ],
          "text": "Aswan High Dam"
        },
        {
          "_key": "vs9micozge48",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": ", the entire island sanctuary was moved. The boat crossing makes the arrival a memorable experience in itself."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [
        {
          "_key": "hos9ez8hysz4",
          "_type": "internalLink",
          "reference": {
            "_ref": "wp-page-59236",
            "_type": "reference"
          }
        }
      ],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "00000000002b",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "adr0d9jupmas",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [
            "mer90gmx28aq"
          ],
          "text": "Elephantine Island"
        },
        {
          "_key": "kl24f3gqvfoo",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": ", "
        },
        {
          "_key": "g0omzxj6ff48",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [
            "ritxetkfrniq"
          ],
          "text": "the Nubian Museum"
        },
        {
          "_key": "k8upnqbaxqps",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": ", the Nubian villages, and the west-bank dunes are also faces of Aswan that matter. Here, Egypt reads not only inside the Arab-pharaonic-Mediterranean frame but also inside a Nubian and African context."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [
        {
          "_key": "mer90gmx28aq",
          "_type": "internalLink",
          "reference": {
            "_ref": "guideArticle.aswan.elephantine-island",
            "_type": "reference"
          }
        },
        {
          "_key": "ritxetkfrniq",
          "_type": "internalLink",
          "reference": {
            "_ref": "guideArticle.aswan.the-nubian-museum",
            "_type": "reference"
          }
        }
      ],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "00000000002d",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "3cz2ynmfgt3y",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [
            "w3eup25bg8sw"
          ],
          "text": "Abu Simbel"
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [
        {
          "_key": "w3eup25bg8sw",
          "_type": "internalLink",
          "reference": {
            "_ref": "wp-page-56813",
            "_type": "reference"
          }
        }
      ],
      "style": "h2"
    },
    {
      "_key": "00000000002f",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "00000000002e",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "Abu Simbel sits further south of Aswan. The distance is real, but the Great Temple of Ramses II and the smaller temple of Nefertari are worth the effort."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000031",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "000000000030",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "What is striking here is not only scale. The story of the temples being cut apart and lifted to higher ground in the 1960s to save them from rising Lake Nasser also matters. They are a monument to ancient kingship and, at the same time, a record of twentieth-century international preservation."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000033",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "000000000032",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "On a shorter trip they get left out. If you reach Aswan, the question of whether to include Abu Simbel is worth taking seriously."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000035",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "000000000034",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "The Red Sea"
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "h2"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000037",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "000000000036",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "Egypt's Red Sea coast is a place you visit for reasons different from the ruins. In Hurghada, Marsa Alam, and Sharm el-Sheikh, reef, clear water, diving, and snorkelling become the centre of the trip."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000039",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "000000000038",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "Hurghada is easy to access and suits family travel and resort stays. Sharm el-Sheikh is strong as the gateway to the Sinai reefs and Ras Mohammed. Marsa Alam is quieter and suits travellers looking for turtles, the chance of dugong, and reef in good condition."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "00000000003b",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "00000000003a",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "Adding the Red Sea changes the density of the trip. It gives rest to a sites-heavy itinerary and reminds you that Egypt is not only the Nile but also a country of the sea."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "00000000003d",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "00000000003c",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "The Western Desert and the oases"
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "h2"
    },
    {
      "_key": "00000000003f",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "00000000003e",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "If your schedule allows, the Western Desert shows another face of Egypt. Bahariya, the Black Desert, the White Desert, Fayoum, Siwa. Each is a different trip from Cairo and Luxor."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000041",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "000000000040",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "Fayoum is comparatively close to Cairo, and you can combine the lake, the desert, the fossil valley of Wadi El Hitan, and the pyramids of Meidum and Hawara. Bahariya and the White Desert are striking for their black volcanic terrain and white wind-carved formations."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000043",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "000000000042",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "Siwa is further still — a destination of several days. Berber-influenced culture, salt lakes, springs, the Shali fortress, and the temple of the Oracle of Alexander. Not a place to consume quickly."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000045",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "000000000044",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "A desert trip is hard to fit into a first short Egypt visit. On a second trip, or one over two weeks, it broadens the understanding considerably."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000047",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "000000000046",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "How to combine them"
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "h2"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000049",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "000000000048",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "For a first trip of one week, the spine is Cairo–Giza and Luxor. If you have margin, add Aswan."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "00000000004b",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "00000000004a",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "With ten to twelve days, you can run Cairo, Luxor, Aswan, Abu Simbel, and close with rest on the Red Sea. That balances ancient Egypt and recovery."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "00000000004d",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "00000000004c",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "With two weeks or more, room appears for the Western Desert, Fayoum, or Siwa. Distances in this country are real; places that look close on a map can take time. Push too hard and the trip turns into transport."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "00000000004f",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "00000000004e",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "Finally"
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "h2"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000051",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "000000000050",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "Choosing what to visit in Egypt is not adding sites. It is deciding which Egypt you want to see."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000053",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "000000000052",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "Giza is strong as a beginning. Luxor gives the depth. Aswan shows the Nile in another register. Abu Simbel marks the far edge of kingship. The Red Sea adds rest and the underwater world. The desert opens the wide margin that lies outside Egypt-as-the-Nile-country."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    },
    {
      "_key": "000000000055",
      "_type": "block",
      "children": [
        {
          "_key": "000000000054",
          "_type": "span",
          "marks": [],
          "text": "You do not need to see everything on one trip. Choose the layer you want and give it enough time — Egypt stays with you better that way."
        }
      ],
      "markDefs": [],
      "style": "normal"
    }
  ],
